WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.970
It was the middle of winter, on an unusually mild midday, when the rivets burst like machine-gun fire, and a brown, sticky wave as tall as a five-story building swept into the street at fifty kilometers an hour.

2
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:17.060
That day in Boston, people drowned not in water, but in sugar.

3
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.510
And the tank had been leaking for years, and everyone knew it.

4
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.960
On the northern edge of Boston Harbor lay an old neighborhood of narrow, winding streets.

5
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.330
It was called the North End.

6
00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:42.900
At the start of the twentieth century, the North End was home largely to Italian immigrants.

7
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.190
Families lived stacked on top of one another in crowded tenements.

8
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:57.880
The streets rang with the dialects of Naples and Sicily, the smell of bread rose from the bakeries each morning, and children ran along the wharves gathering scraps of wood to burn in the stove at night.

9
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:04.410
Right beside the neighborhood, running parallel to the harbor, was a broad thoroughfare: Commercial Street.

10
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.540
Above it, on iron legs, stretched an elevated railway.

11
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:16.990
Trains rumbled past all day long, while below, horse-drawn wagons, trucks, and laborers hauling freight came and went.

12
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:25.920
In nineteen fifteen, on the harbor side of Commercial Street, at lot number five twenty-nine, something new was added to the neighborhood's skyline.

13
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:33.570
It was a giant cylindrical tank built of riveted steel plates, roughly fifteen meters tall and twenty-seven meters across.

14
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.020
It could hold eight point seven million liters of molasses.

15
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:45.510
Molasses is the dark, heavy, sticky syrup left over after sugarcane is processed and the sugar is extracted.

16
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:52.200
It may rarely appear in kitchens today, but in the America of that era it mattered both in food and in industry.

17
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.810
When molasses is fermented, it yields ethyl alcohol.

18
00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:02.220
That alcohol could be used to make liquor, but its truly valuable use lay elsewhere.

19
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:08.750
Industrial alcohol was an essential raw material in the production of smokeless gunpowder and other explosives.

20
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.240
The tank belonged to the Purity Distilling Company.

21
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:19.250
That company, in turn, was a subsidiary of a much larger firm, United States Industrial Alcohol.

22
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.300
The plan was simple.

23
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:33.110
Molasses shipped in from the Caribbean, especially from Cuba and Puerto Rico, would be unloaded into the tank at Boston Harbor, then carried by rail to the company's distillery in Cambridge, just outside Boston,

24
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.640
to be turned into alcohol.

25
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.010
The timing was no accident.

26
00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.140
In Europe, the First World War had been raging for a year.

27
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.870
The United States had not yet entered the war, but it was selling munitions to Britain and France.

28
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:56.000
Thousands of artillery shells were being fired at the front every day, and every shell needed explosives, and every explosive needed alcohol.

29
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.770
Prices for industrial alcohol were climbing, and demand kept rising.

30
00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.860
United States Industrial Alcohol wanted to move fast to meet that demand.

31
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.310
A large tank to store molasses was the shortest route to profit.

32
00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.320
Nor was the tank's location an accident.

33
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:20.010
It sat right on the waterfront, where ships could dock directly alongside, and the rail line was close by.

34
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.180
But the site had a social dimension as well.

35
00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.750
The North End was one of Boston's poorest neighborhoods, and one of its least politically vocal.

36
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:34.960
Many of its residents were not yet citizens, could not speak English well, and knew no one at City Hall.

37
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:43.410
In Boston's wealthy districts, there would have been lawyers, journalists, and city councilors ready to object to a fifteen-meter steel tank.

38
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.380
In the North End, there were none.

39
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:52.950
Most historians believe that this silence played a part in the decision to build the tank there.

40
00:03:52.950 --> 00:04:02.840
And so, in the autumn of nineteen fifteen, driven by the lure of wartime profits and emboldened by the neighborhood's silence, the steel giant rose into place.

41
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.050
The neighbors grew used to it.

42
00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.500
But before long, they began to notice strange things about the tank.

43
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.630
The man responsible for building the tank was named Arthur Jell.

44
00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:19.680
Jell was the Purity Distilling Company's treasurer, the manager in charge of its accounts and finances.

45
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.490
He was not an engineer, and he was not an architect.

46
00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:28.100
As would later come out in court, he could not even read a technical drawing.

47
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:33.990
Even so, the company had handed him the job of overseeing this enormous steel structure.

48
00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:41.480
Jell had the tank built by a firm called Hammond Iron Works, and he never sought an independent engineering opinion from anyone.

49
00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:48.170
Not a single expert looked over the tank's plans, and no one calculated the load the steel plates would have to bear.

50
00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.980
Jell was under heavy pressure from the clock.

51
00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:59.790
A ship carrying molasses from the Caribbean was due to reach the harbor by a certain date, and the company wanted the tank ready when it arrived.

52
00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.960
Construction was crammed into the final weeks.

53
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:11.090
For a tank like this, it was standard practice to fill it completely with water before putting it into service, to test it for leaks and for strength.

54
00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.740
But this tank was never filled to the brim with water.

55
00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:22.550
According to later testimony, only about fifteen centimeters of water was poured into the bottom, and that test was deemed sufficient.

56
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.880
Then the first load of molasses was pumped in.

57
00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.090
Problems appeared right away.

58
00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.900
The tank began leaking from the very first time it was filled.

59
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:39.870
Brown molasses seeped drop by drop between the rivets and through the seams where the plates met, leaving long dark streaks down the side of the tank.

60
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.360
The company's answer to this was a curious one.

61
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.490
Rather than repairing the leaks, it painted the tank brown.

62
00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:53.060
That way, the oozing molasses blended into the color of the steel and was far less noticeable.

63
00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:58.630
The people of the neighborhood knew about the leaks, and they made use of them in their own way.

64
00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:04.520
Poor families came to the foot of the tank with buckets and pots to collect the molasses that dripped out.

65
00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.490
Children dipped their fingers into it and licked them clean.

66
00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.620
At home, it was used to make sweets or added to meals.

67
00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:18.190
No one thought of it as a warning sign, or if anyone did, they found no one to tell.

68
00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:22.960
It was said that the tank did not only leak, but also made noises.

69
00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:30.930
Isaac Gonzales, one of the workers responsible for its upkeep, described hearing deep groans and creaks coming from the tank when it was full.

70
00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:38.260
He said he was so worried that he could not sleep at night for fear the tank would collapse, and that on some nights he got up and went to check on it.

71
00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:42.070
Gonzales reported what he had seen to Arthur Jell.

72
00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:45.720
Jell, however, did not take him seriously.

73
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:53.690
According to later testimony, Jell closed the matter by telling Gonzales that he did not understand the business, and that the tank was still standing.

74
00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.180
Some time later, Gonzales left the job.

75
00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.190
The root of the problem lay in the steel.

76
00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:08.640
Nearly a hundred years later, in twenty fourteen, the engineer Ronald Mayville took a fresh look at the old documents and the tank's specifications.

77
00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.490
He concluded that the tank's steel plates were thinner than they should have been for a tank meant to hold a liquid of that volume and that weight.

78
00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:21.860
What is more, the steel was low in manganese, the element that gives steel its toughness and flexibility.

79
00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.870
Steel like this becomes brittle in cold weather.

80
00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:31.400
In other words, under impact or strain, instead of flexing and bending, it can crack like glass.

81
00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:36.970
Mayville also suspected that small cracks may have formed around the rivet holes and grown over time.

82
00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:43.500
These cracks were most dangerous at the very bottom of the tank, the place that bore the weight of all the molasses above it.

83
00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.830
And molasses is no ordinary liquid.

84
00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.160
It is roughly forty percent heavier than water.

85
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:58.290
The pressure bearing down on the floor and lower walls of a full tank was far greater than it would have been had the same tank been filled with water.

86
00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.940
And the tank was constantly being filled and emptied.

87
00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:07.830
Each time it was filled, the steel stretched, and each time it was emptied, it relaxed.

88
00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:13.240
This rising and falling load was slowly wearing out plates that were already weak.

89
00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:17.690
In April of nineteen seventeen, the United States entered the war.

90
00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.180
Demand for industrial alcohol rose even higher.

91
00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:26.590
In November of nineteen eighteen the war came to an end, but the tank was still hard at work.

92
00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:30.400
This time, a different development was on the horizon.

93
00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:38.210
A constitutional amendment that would ban alcoholic beverages across the country was being ratified by the states, one after another.

94
00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:47.460
Some historians argue that the company wanted to produce and sell as much alcohol as possible before the ban took effect, and that this is why it filled the tank to the brim.

95
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.590
This has never been conclusively proven, but the timing is striking.

96
00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:59.240
In the first days of January nineteen nineteen, a ship arriving from the Caribbean unloaded a fresh cargo of molasses.

97
00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.090
The tank was almost completely full.

98
00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:06.060
It held roughly eight point seven million liters of molasses.

99
00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.550
And the weather was bitterly cold.

100
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:15.600
On the fourteenth of January, the temperature in Boston had dropped as low as minus seventeen degrees.

101
00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.490
On the morning of the fifteenth of January, nineteen nineteen, the weather changed suddenly.

102
00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:28.820
The temperature, which had been minus seventeen degrees the day before, climbed toward noon to plus four or five degrees.

103
00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:33.110
After a long cold spell, it felt like spring.

104
00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:36.600
People unbuttoned their coats and lingered outdoors.

105
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.610
Commercial Street was as busy as ever.

106
00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:46.460
City workers were working on the road, dockworkers were hauling cargo, horse-drawn wagons and trucks rolled past.

107
00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:50.750
In the canteen of one of the city buildings, people were eating lunch.

108
00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:57.280
At Fire Station Number Thirty-One, near the tank, firefighters were playing cards.

109
00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:02.690
Children, as usual, were gathering scraps of firewood beside the railway tracks.

110
00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:07.460
At around twelve forty, there came a sound that the witnesses would never forget.

111
00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.830
Some described a deep rumble.

112
00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:15.400
Others said they heard a rapid series of sharp cracks, like a machine gun firing.

113
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:20.810
These sounds were the rivets, snapping off and shooting out one after another.

114
00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:24.460
The steel plates were tearing apart.

115
00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:30.990
Then the body of the tank split open, and the millions of liters of molasses inside poured out all at once.

116
00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.960
When a tank leaks, the liquid flows out slowly.

117
00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:40.050
But this tank did not open slowly; it burst apart almost in an instant.

118
00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.540
What emerged was not a stream, but a wave.

119
00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:53.590
According to the witnesses' accounts and later calculations, the wave of molasses reached seven point five meters at its highest point and moved at roughly fifty-five kilometers per hour.

120
00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:57.720
In other words, faster than a running person could escape.

121
00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:02.010
The torn steel pieces of the tank were flung in every direction.

122
00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:08.380
One of the large plates struck the iron supports of the elevated railway above the street and bent the structure.

123
00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.230
The tracks sagged.

124
00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.400
At that very moment, a train was approaching.

125
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.970
When the motorman saw the tracks collapsing ahead of him, he stopped the train at the last moment.

126
00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.780
Another train had passed by there only moments before.

127
00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:28.710
By a matter of seconds, one disaster was spared from having another added to it.

128
00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:32.520
The wave struck the buildings right beside the tank first.

129
00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.010
Wooden houses were torn from their foundations.

130
00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:42.860
One structure, it was said, was shoved with such force that it seemed to be ripped from its place and flung aside.

131
00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.830
Fire Station Number Thirty-One was knocked off its footing.

132
00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:52.080
Molasses flooded the inside of the station, and part of the building collapsed.

133
00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.850
The firefighters inside were trapped beneath the wreckage and the molasses.

134
00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:02.100
What made molasses so dangerous as a liquid was its weight and its stickiness.

135
00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:08.470
A person swept away by water can try to swim, to grab hold of something, to keep their head above the surface.

136
00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.440
Someone caught in a wave of molasses could do none of that.

137
00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:16.570
The more they moved their arms and legs, the deeper they sank.

138
00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:21.500
Molasses filled their mouths and noses, making it impossible to breathe.

139
00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:25.310
Horses and wagons were dragged along inside the wave.

140
00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:30.400
Street workers, dock workers, and passersby were all swept away.

141
00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.170
Children gathering firewood along the railway tracks were also in the wave's path.

142
00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:39.780
Ten-year-old Pasquale Iantosca was caught by the wave and killed.

143
00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:46.150
Maria Di Stasio, also ten years old, had been gathering wood with her siblings.

144
00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:49.320
She, too, did not survive.

145
00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:55.210
Some children, though, were caught at the edge of the wave and carried along on its crest, and they lived.

146
00:12:55.210 --> 00:13:01.900
One survivor described being carried on top of the wave like a surfer, and then simply set down somewhere.

147
00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:05.230
The wave did not only move forward.

148
00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:09.840
The molasses spread into the narrow streets and flowed toward the wharf and the harbor.

149
00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:15.730
In some places, within just a few minutes, a sticky layer nearly one meter thick had formed.

150
00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:19.700
And that layer was changing fast.

151
00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:23.670
The weather may have turned mild, but it was still January.

152
00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:30.200
The warm molasses pouring out of the tank cooled as it met the cold air, and as it cooled, it grew thicker still.

153
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:38.010
When the wave finally came to rest, what it left behind was a swamp that kept hardening, seizing and holding fast to everything that fell into it.

154
00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.180
All of this happened within a few minutes.

155
00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:44.990
At twelve forty, it had been an ordinary lunch hour.

156
00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:51.520
A few minutes later, a stretch of Commercial Street had become a field of sticky brown wreckage.

157
00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.530
The first help arrived very quickly.

158
00:13:54.530 --> 00:14:00.580
Near the tank, a training ship lay moored in the harbor: the USS Nantucket.

159
00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:05.670
When the naval cadets aboard heard the sound of the blast, they came running to the scene.

160
00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:13.160
The Boston police, the fire department, soldiers from the army, and Red Cross volunteers soon reached the area as well.

161
00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:19.850
But once the rescuers set to work, they realized they were facing something none of them had ever encountered before.

162
00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:23.660
The molasses also trapped anyone who stepped into it.

163
00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:29.550
Rescuers struggled forward, sunk to their knees, and in some places to their waists.

164
00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:31.920
Every step was a fight.

165
00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:37.330
To pull the injured free, the molasses had to be scraped away by hand and with shovels.

166
00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:42.580
Voices and groans could be heard from beneath the wreckage, along with the screams of horses.

167
00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:47.670
Many of the horses, injured and stuck fast in the molasses, could not be saved.

168
00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:52.760
Police and soldiers were forced to shoot them so they would not suffer.

169
00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:58.010
One of the longest and hardest rescue efforts took place at Fire Station Number Thirty-One.

170
00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:03.260
Firefighters worked for hours to reach their comrades trapped beneath the collapsed building.

171
00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:08.030
Some of those caught under the wreckage along with the molasses were rescued.

172
00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:13.280
But firefighter George Layhe was pinned beneath the collapsed part of the building.

173
00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:18.210
His fellow firefighters heard his voice and tried to reach him, but they could not.

174
00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:21.540
Layhe drowned in the molasses that surrounded him.

175
00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.710
His body could only be recovered hours later.

176
00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:28.040
The injured were carried to nearby hospitals.

177
00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:32.170
The Red Cross turned a building in the neighborhood into a temporary hospital.

178
00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:36.140
Doctors and nurses now faced a problem they were not used to.

179
00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:40.910
Before they could examine their patients, they first had to clean the molasses off them.

180
00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:45.840
It clung to clothing, to hair, to skin, and it covered the wounds.

181
00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:51.730
Some patients could not be made to breathe until the molasses had been cleared from their mouths and noses.

182
00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:55.380
The search went on without pause for four days.

183
00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:59.190
As the molasses hardened, the work grew even harder.

184
00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:04.120
Some of the victims' bodies were so coated in molasses that they were difficult to identify.

185
00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:08.090
For others, weeks went by before they were found at all.

186
00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:13.820
The bodies of some victims who had been swept into the harbor were only pulled from the water months later.

187
00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.670
In the end, the toll was confirmed.

188
00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:21.920
Twenty-one people had lost their lives, and around one hundred fifty had been injured.

189
00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:26.690
Among the dead were ten-year-old children, and elderly laborers too.

190
00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:32.100
City workers, dock workers, wagon drivers, a firefighter.

191
00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:39.110
Most of them lived in or near the North End, ordinary people doing their jobs or simply passing by at an ordinary lunch hour.

192
00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:45.000
Beyond burying the dead and healing the injured, there was another task just as hard: the cleanup.

193
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.970
Molasses had spread across every part of Commercial Street.

194
00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:57.100
The streets, the houses, the shops, the supports of the elevated railway, and the wharves of the harbor were all coated in a brown layer.

195
00:16:57.100 --> 00:17:01.870
At first they tried washing it away with fresh water, but that did not work.

196
00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:06.800
Eventually they found that the most effective method was to use water from the harbor.

197
00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:12.370
Fireboats pumped salt water from the harbor and sprayed it onto the streets at high pressure.

198
00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:16.500
The salt water broke down and softened the molasses.

199
00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:20.150
Then workers mixed the molasses with sand and scraped it away.

200
00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.520
The job took weeks.

201
00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:27.450
In all, it is estimated that the cleanup took eighty-seven thousand man-hours of work.

202
00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:30.940
Molasses did not stay at the scene of the disaster.

203
00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:37.950
Rescuers, reporters and curious onlookers carried it across the entire city on their shoes and their clothing.

204
00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:44.480
Sticky traces turned up in streetcars, in telephone booths, on the door knockers of people's homes.

205
00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:49.250
In the months after the disaster, the water of Boston Harbor looked brown.

206
00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:56.100
According to some accounts, it was not until summer arrived that the water finally returned to its normal color.

207
00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:59.430
And then there was the smell.

208
00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:07.880
For decades, longtime residents of the North End would describe how, on hot summer days, a sweetish scent of molasses rose up through the neighborhood.

209
00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:15.210
The molasses had seeped between the streets, into the foundations of old buildings, beneath the wooden floorboards.

210
00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:19.180
The neighborhood remembered its catastrophe through the smell.

211
00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:25.710
But amid all this suffering, Boston's real question had yet to be answered: why had the tank collapsed?

212
00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.080
And who was responsible?

213
00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:33.010
The United States Industrial Alcohol Company had its answer ready.

214
00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:37.460
According to the company, the tank had not simply collapsed on its own.

215
00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.830
Someone had bombed it.

216
00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:44.440
In the atmosphere of those days, the claim did not sound strange at all.

217
00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:51.130
In the immediate aftermath of the First World War, fear of political violence ran deep across America.

218
00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:57.980
The revolution in Russia, strikes among industrial workers, and anarchist groups dominated the headlines.

219
00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:04.830
The followers of the Italian anarchist Luigi Galleani in particular were known in those years for their bombing campaigns.

220
00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:09.120
And the North End itself was a neighborhood of Italian immigrants.

221
00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:18.850
The company argued that the tank had been used for industrial alcohol, in other words for the production of explosives, and that this could have made it a target for anti-war anarchists.

222
00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:22.500
For the company, it was a very convenient explanation.

223
00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:28.070
If the blame could be placed on an outside enemy, no one would ever ask the question of negligence.

224
00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:33.000
The families affected by the disaster, however, did not accept that explanation.

225
00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:39.050
Relatives of the dead, the injured, and those who had lost their homes and livelihoods sued the company.

226
00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:46.220
The lawsuits were combined and became one of the first major class action cases in the state of Massachusetts.

227
00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.390
There were more than one hundred plaintiffs.

228
00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:54.960
To hear and weigh the evidence in this complex case, the court appointed an independent auditor.

229
00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:58.130
He was a respected attorney named Hugh Ogden.

230
00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:01.940
The hearings Ogden conducted went on for years.

231
00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:05.110
Nearly three thousand witnesses testified.

232
00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:09.080
The result was tens of thousands of pages of testimony.

233
00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:12.730
The courtroom turned into an engineering lecture hall.

234
00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:19.580
The company's experts argued that the tank had been sound and could only have collapsed the way it did because of an explosion.

235
00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:29.630
The plaintiffs' experts, on the other hand, explained that the tank had given way under its own weight, that the steel was inadequate, and that the rivets and plates could not bear the load.

236
00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:34.240
Over the course of the hearings, the story of the tank came to light.

237
00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:40.770
When Arthur Jell admitted on the witness stand that he could not read a technical drawing, it caused a sensation.

238
00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:44.580
It emerged that the tank had never been fully tested with water.

239
00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:54.470
The leaks, the paint used to hide them, the neighborhood residents collecting molasses in buckets, Isaac Gonzales's warnings — one by one, they all entered the record.

240
00:20:54.470 --> 00:21:01.640
Gonzales himself testified as well, describing the sounds the tank used to make and how his warnings had been received.

241
00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:08.170
The company's lawyers tried to cast doubt on his credibility, but his account matched the testimony of the other witnesses.

242
00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:12.460
The bombing claim, meanwhile, rested on no concrete evidence.

243
00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:19.310
No trace of explosives had been found at the scene, and there was neither an attacker nor any group claiming responsibility.

244
00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:25.840
The "machine-gun fire" that witnesses had heard could be explained not as a bomb, but as the sound of rivets snapping.

245
00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:30.130
In nineteen twenty-five, Hugh Ogden released his report.

246
00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:35.860
His conclusion was unequivocal: the tank had not been built strongly enough for the load it carried.

247
00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:41.270
Its design was inadequate, its steel was inadequate, and it had never been inspected.

248
00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:45.880
As for the bombing claim, he rejected it for lack of supporting evidence.

249
00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:51.610
Ogden held the United States Industrial Alcohol Company responsible for the disaster.

250
00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:57.980
The company agreed to pay the plaintiffs a total of roughly six hundred twenty-eight thousand dollars in damages.

251
00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:03.070
The share for the families of those who had died came to about seven thousand dollars per person.

252
00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:10.240
In the money of the day, that was no small sum, but it was just as clear that it could never take the place of a child or a father.

253
00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:15.970
The true significance of the case, however, lay less in the compensation than in the message it sent.

254
00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:23.300
A powerful corporation had been defeated, after years in court, by the people of a neighborhood far weaker than itself.

255
00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:27.910
And that defeat rested squarely on a question of engineering.

256
00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:31.560
Money and land were not enough to put up a structure.

257
00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:37.290
It took an expert who knew the structure was safe and who would vouch for it with their own signature.

258
00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:44.140
In the years following the disaster, Boston and the state of Massachusetts turned this lesson into rules.

259
00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:54.190
It became mandatory for the construction plans of large structures to be calculated and approved by a licensed engineer or architect, and for those plans to bear that expert's seal.

260
00:22:54.190 --> 00:23:02.960
It was no longer legally possible for a manager who could not read a building's drawings to put up an eight point seven million liter tank without consulting anyone.

261
00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:07.410
Similar regulations spread to other states over the following decades.

262
00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:18.580
Today, no one in America finds it strange that construction plans require an engineer's approval, but the Boston molasses disaster is remembered as an important turning point in establishing that practice.

263
00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:21.750
The timing of the disaster held a certain irony.

264
00:23:21.750 --> 00:23:32.920
On January sixteenth, nineteen nineteen, the day after the collapse of January fifteenth, the constitutional amendment banning alcoholic beverages reached the number of states required for ratification.

265
00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:37.530
With that, it became final, and it took effect one year later.

266
00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:43.580
A tank built to turn molasses into alcohol had burst on the very eve of the day alcohol was outlawed.

267
00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:48.510
The science behind the disaster also stirred curiosity for many years.

268
00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:55.520
Witnesses described how the molasses could seize and hold a person so quickly, and why rescuers struggled so hard.

269
00:23:55.520 --> 00:24:06.050
In twenty sixteen, Nicole Sharp, a fluid dynamics researcher from Harvard University, and a student, Jordan Scott, set out to examine this question scientifically.

270
00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:11.300
They tested molasses at different temperatures and modeled the conditions of that day.

271
00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:19.590
Their conclusion was this: inside the tank, the molasses had been relatively fluid, thanks to the warming air and a recently added load.

272
00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:24.680
Once it escaped the tank, however, it was quickly exposed to the cold air.

273
00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:30.250
As it cooled, its fluidity dropped sharply, and the molasses grew thicker and thicker.

274
00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:34.380
For those caught in the wave, the result was horrifying.

275
00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:44.270
The liquid that surged forward in the first seconds, sweeping people along, had within a few minutes thickened almost like cement, leaving those trapped inside unable to move.

276
00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:48.080
This also explained why the rescuers had struggled so much.

277
00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:52.530
Today, where the tank once stood on Commercial Street, there is a park.

278
00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:58.900
A baseball field, a children's playground, and courts where older residents play bocce.

279
00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:07.350
At the entrance to the park, a small plaque put up in nineteen ninety-seven recalls the disaster and those who died.

280
00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:17.080
In twenty nineteen, the hundredth anniversary of the disaster, memorial ceremonies were held in Boston, and the names of the twenty-one victims were read aloud.

281
00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:26.330
An event that for years had been remembered as a neighborhood tale, and sometimes even as the subject of a joke, was at last remembered again with the seriousness it deserved.

282
00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:32.220
At first glance, the Boston molasses flood looks like a strange footnote in history.

283
00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:35.710
A sugar syrup that swallowed part of a city.

284
00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:47.840
But behind this strangeness lies a very familiar story: shortcuts taken for quick profit, warnings ignored, leaks hidden from view, and a negligence whose price was paid by the quietest people of all.

285
00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:56.130
The brown wave that covered Commercial Street that day receded, the streets were washed down with salt water, and in the end even the smell faded away.

286
00:25:56.130 --> 00:26:01.380
What remained is the engineer's signature found today on the plans of every major structure.

287
00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:08.230
That signature owes a great deal to Boston's twenty-one dead, and to the families who sought justice for them for years.

288
00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:12.040
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

289
00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:14.730
Until we meet again in a new story.
